---
import BackgroundMarquee from "../../../components/BackgroundMarquee.astro";
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="Ahmet Tarik Duyar - Blog Explorations" description="10 Integrated Blog Section Designs" bodyClass="bg-[#0a0b0d] text-white w-screen overflow-x-hidden">
	<main class="bg-[#0d0e12] border-x border-[#90c5ff14] max-w-7xl mx-auto min-h-dvh relative">
		<!-- Subtle noise overlay -->
		<div class="fixed inset-0 pointer-events-none opacity-[0.015] z-50" style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg viewBox=%220 0 256 256%22 xmlns=%22http://www.w3.org/2000/svg%22%3E%3Cfilter id=%22n%22%3E%3CfeTurbulence type=%22fractalNoise%22 baseFrequency=%220.9%22 numOctaves=%224%22 stitchTiles=%22stitch%22/%3E%3C/filter%3E%3Crect width=%22100%25%22 height=%22100%25%22 filter=%22url(%23n)%22/%3E%3C/svg%3E');"></div>
		
		<!-- Navigation -->
		<nav class="py-6 px-4 md:px-8 border-b border-[#90c5ff14] sticky top-0 bg-[#0d0e12]/90 backdrop-blur-sm z-40">
			<div class="flex justify-between items-center">
				<a href="#hero" class="group flex items-center gap-3">
					<div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-full border border-blue-400/30 flex items-center justify-center group-hover:border-blue-400/60 transition-colors relative overflow-hidden">
						<div class="absolute inset-0 bg-blue-500/10 group-hover:bg-blue-500/20 transition-colors"></div>
						<img src="/favicon.svg" alt="Logo" class="w-6 h-6 relative z-10" />
					</div>
					<span class="font-sinistre text-xl tracking-wide hidden sm:block">Atduyar</span>
				</a>
				<div class="flex items-center gap-4">
					<span class="text-xs text-gray-500 font-mono hidden md:block">INTEGRATED DESIGNS</span>
					<span class="text-xs text-blue-400/60 font-mono">/final/14</span>
				</div>
			</div>
		</nav>

		<!-- Hero Section -->
		<section id="hero" class="min-h-[50vh] flex flex-col justify-center px-4 md:px-8 lg:px-16 py-16 border-b border-[#90c5ff14] relative overflow-hidden">
			<div class="absolute inset-0">
				<div class="absolute top-1/4 left-1/4 w-[500px] h-[500px] bg-blue-500/10 rounded-full blur-[150px]"></div>
			</div>
			
			<div class="relative z-10">
				<p class="font-oldturcic text-blue-400/50 text-lg mb-4 tracking-[0.4em] leading-none">𐰚𐰇𐰠𐱅𐰃𐰏𐰤</p>
				<h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl lg:text-6xl font-sinistre leading-[0.9] mb-6">
					<span class="text-blue-300">Integrated</span> Blog Designs
				</h1>
				<p class="text-lg text-gray-400 max-w-xl leading-relaxed">
					10 layouts that feel native to this site. Breaking containers, asymmetric compositions, typography-focused UX.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>


		<!-- ============================================== -->
		<!-- BLOG 1: THE BLEED -->
		<!-- Full-width posts that break out of container -->
		<!-- ============================================== -->
		<section class="relative">
			<!-- Section label - floating -->
			<div class="absolute left-4 md:left-8 top-8 z-20">
				<div class="flex items-center gap-3">
					<span class="font-oldturcic text-xl text-blue-400/30">𐰃</span>
					<span class="font-sinistre text-sm text-gray-500 tracking-wider">THE BLEED</span>
				</div>
			</div>
			
			<!-- Featured post - bleeds to edges -->
			<article class="pt-24 pb-0">
				<!-- Full-width gradient bar -->
				<div class="h-px w-full bg-gradient-to-r from-transparent via-blue-500/40 to-transparent"></div>
				
				<!-- Post content that bleeds -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-12 min-h-[70vh]">
					<!-- Left bleed area with accent -->
					<div class="col-span-12 lg:col-span-5 bg-gradient-to-br from-blue-950/40 via-blue-900/20 to-transparent relative">
						<div class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-r from-blue-500/5 to-transparent"></div>
						<div class="sticky top-24 p-8 lg:p-12 h-fit">
							<div class="text-xs text-blue-400/60 font-mono mb-4">2026.02.09</div>
							<h2 class="font-sinistre text-3xl lg:text-4xl xl:text-5xl leading-[1.1] mb-6 text-white">
								Why I Stopped Using ORMs and Started Writing SQL Again
							</h2>
							<p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-6">
								After years of abstracting away the database, I returned to raw SQL. Here's what I learned about control, performance, and understanding your data.
							</p>
							<div class="flex items-center gap-4 text-sm text-gray-500">
								<span>12 min read</span>
								<span class="w-1 h-1 rounded-full bg-gray-600"></span>
								<span>Database</span>
							</div>
						</div>
					</div>
					
					<!-- Right content area -->
					<div class="col-span-12 lg:col-span-7 p-8 lg:p-12 lg:pl-16 border-l border-[#90c5ff14]">
						<div class="prose prose-invert max-w-none">
							<p class="text-lg text-gray-300 leading-relaxed first-letter:text-5xl first-letter:font-sinistre first-letter:text-blue-300 first-letter:float-left first-letter:mr-3 first-letter:mt-1">
								The abstraction was comfortable. ActiveRecord, Prisma, SQLAlchemy they all promised the same thing: write less SQL, think in objects, let the framework handle the messy details. And for years, I believed them.
							</p>
							<p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mt-6">
								Then I inherited a system where a single API endpoint took 8 seconds to respond. The ORM had generated a query with 14 JOINs, none of them indexed properly. The fix took 30 minutes of writing raw SQL and understanding the actual data relationships.
							</p>
							<blockquote class="border-l-2 border-blue-500/50 pl-6 my-8 text-xl text-blue-100/80 italic">
								"The database is not an implementation detail. It's the foundation of your system."
							</blockquote>
							<p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
								This isn't an argument against all abstraction. It's an argument for understanding what you're abstracting. When you write SQL, you think about indexes. You think about query plans. You think about data access patterns. The ORM hides these concerns until they become emergencies.
							</p>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>
			</article>
			
			<div class="h-px w-full bg-gradient-to-r from-transparent via-blue-500/20 to-transparent"></div>
		</section>


		<!-- ============================================== -->
		<!-- BLOG 2: THE RIVER -->
		<!-- Flowing content with no hard boundaries -->
		<!-- ============================================== -->
		<section class="relative py-32">
			<!-- Floating section indicator -->
			<div class="absolute right-4 md:right-8 top-8">
				<div class="flex items-center gap-3">
					<span class="font-sinistre text-sm text-gray-500 tracking-wider">THE RIVER</span>
					<span class="font-oldturcic text-xl text-blue-400/30">𐰃𐰃</span>
				</div>
			</div>
			
			<!-- Continuous flowing content -->
			<div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 md:px-8">
				<!-- Post flows into post with date markers -->
				<div class="relative">
					<!-- Flowing vertical line -->
					<div class="absolute left-0 top-0 bottom-0 w-px bg-gradient-to-b from-transparent via-[#90c5ff20] to-transparent"></div>
					
					<!-- Entry 1 -->
					<div class="pl-8 mb-24 relative">
						<div class="absolute left-0 top-2 w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-blue-400/60 -translate-x-1/2"></div>
						<time class="text-xs text-blue-400/50 font-mono">February 2026</time>
						<h3 class="font-sinistre text-2xl mt-2 mb-4 text-white hover:text-blue-200 transition-colors cursor-pointer">
							The Borrow Checker Changed How I Think
						</h3>
						<p class="text-gray-400 leading-loose">
							Six months into Rust, something shifted. I started seeing ownership patterns everywhere even in languages without a borrow checker. Python code that felt fine now revealed hidden dangers. JavaScript callbacks triggered mental alarms about dangling references. The compiler had rewired my brain.
						</p>
					</div>
					
					<!-- Entry 2 - flows naturally from 1 -->
					<div class="pl-8 mb-24 relative">
						<div class="absolute left-0 top-2 w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-blue-400/40 -translate-x-1/2"></div>
						<time class="text-xs text-blue-400/50 font-mono">January 2026</time>
						<h3 class="font-sinistre text-2xl mt-2 mb-4 text-white hover:text-blue-200 transition-colors cursor-pointer">
							Debugging at 3 AM: A Meditation
						</h3>
						<p class="text-gray-400 leading-loose">
							There's a particular clarity that comes at 3 AM. The world is quiet. Slack is silent. The only sounds are the hum of your machine and the occasional tick of the radiator. In this space, bugs reveal themselves differently. You're not fighting the problem you're sitting with it.
						</p>
						<p class="text-gray-500 leading-loose mt-4 pl-6 border-l border-[#90c5ff14]">
							I've solved more production incidents in these hours than in any daytime debugging session. There's something about the stillness that lets you see patterns you'd otherwise miss...
						</p>
					</div>
					
					<!-- Entry 3 -->
					<div class="pl-8 mb-24 relative">
						<div class="absolute left-0 top-2 w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-blue-400/30 -translate-x-1/2"></div>
						<time class="text-xs text-blue-400/50 font-mono">December 2025</time>
						<h3 class="font-sinistre text-2xl mt-2 mb-4 text-white hover:text-blue-200 transition-colors cursor-pointer">
							On Simplicity and Complexity
						</h3>
						<p class="text-gray-400 leading-loose">
							The senior engineers I admire most share one trait: they make complex things simple. Not by ignoring complexity, but by understanding it so deeply they can explain it in plain terms. Simplicity isn't the absence of complexity it's complexity that's been fully digested.
						</p>
					</div>
					
					<!-- Entry 4 - fading into the past -->
					<div class="pl-8 relative opacity-60">
						<div class="absolute left-0 top-2 w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-blue-400/20 -translate-x-1/2"></div>
						<time class="text-xs text-blue-400/40 font-mono">November 2025</time>
						<h3 class="font-sinistre text-xl mt-2 mb-3 text-gray-300 hover:text-blue-200 transition-colors cursor-pointer">
							First Kernel Module: A Journey
						</h3>
						<p class="text-gray-500 leading-loose text-sm">
							The moment it loaded without crashing the system, I understood why people spend careers in this space...
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>


		<!-- ============================================== -->
		<!-- BLOG 3: THE OVERLAP -->
		<!-- Stacked cards with depth and shadow -->
		<!-- ============================================== -->
		<section class="py-20 px-4 md:px-8 lg:px-16 relative">
			<div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-16">
				<span class="font-oldturcic text-xl text-blue-400/30">𐰃𐰃𐰃</span>
				<span class="font-sinistre text-sm text-gray-500 tracking-wider">THE OVERLAP</span>
				<span class="flex-1 h-px bg-gradient-to-r from-[#90c5ff14] to-transparent"></span>
			</div>
			
			<!-- Overlapping card stack -->
			<div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto relative" style="height: 600px;">
				<!-- Card 3 (back) -->
				<article class="absolute left-[10%] top-[15%] w-[70%] bg-[#12141a] border border-[#90c5ff14] p-8 shadow-2xl transform rotate-[-2deg] hover:rotate-0 hover:scale-[1.02] transition-all duration-300 cursor-pointer z-10">
					<div class="text-xs text-gray-600 font-mono mb-3">Technical Deep Dive</div>
					<h3 class="font-sinistre text-xl text-gray-400 mb-3">Building a Lock-Free Queue in Rust</h3>
					<p class="text-gray-600 text-sm leading-relaxed">
						Atomic operations, memory ordering, and the subtle art of concurrent data structures...
					</p>
				</article>
				
				<!-- Card 2 (middle) -->
				<article class="absolute left-[5%] top-[25%] w-[75%] bg-[#0f1116] border border-[#90c5ff20] p-10 shadow-2xl transform rotate-[1deg] hover:rotate-0 hover:scale-[1.02] transition-all duration-300 cursor-pointer z-20">
					<div class="text-xs text-blue-400/50 font-mono mb-3">Architecture</div>
					<h3 class="font-sinistre text-2xl text-gray-300 mb-4">Event Sourcing: Two Years Later</h3>
					<p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
						We rebuilt our core system around event sourcing. The good, the bad, and the migrations that haunt my dreams.
					</p>
					<div class="mt-6 flex items-center gap-4 text-sm text-gray-600">
						<span>15 min</span>
						<span class="w-1 h-1 rounded-full bg-gray-700"></span>
						<span>2025</span>
					</div>
				</article>
				
				<!-- Card 1 (front - featured) -->
				<article class="absolute left-0 top-[35%] w-[80%] bg-gradient-to-br from-[#0d0f14] to-[#0a0c10] border border-blue-500/20 p-12 shadow-[0_25px_80px_-15px_rgba(0,100,255,0.15)] transform rotate-[-1deg] hover:rotate-0 hover:scale-[1.02] transition-all duration-300 cursor-pointer z-30 group">
					<div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-4">
						<div class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-blue-400"></div>
						<span class="text-xs text-blue-400/70 font-mono">Featured</span>
					</div>
					<h3 class="font-sinistre text-3xl text-white mb-4 group-hover:text-blue-100 transition-colors">
						The Hidden Cost of Microservices
					</h3>
					<p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed text-lg">
						We split our monolith into 23 services. Our deployment time went from 5 minutes to 2 hours. This is the story of how we found the balance.
					</p>
					<div class="mt-8 flex items-center justify-between">
						<div class="flex items-center gap-4 text-sm text-gray-500">
							<span>20 min read</span>
							<span class="w-1 h-1 rounded-full bg-gray-600"></span>
							<span>January 2026</span>
						</div>
						<div class="text-blue-400/60 group-hover:text-blue-400 transition-colors font-sinistre text-sm">
							Read more
						</div>
					</div>
				</article>
			</div>
		</section>


		<!-- ============================================== -->
		<!-- BLOG 4: THE SPLIT -->
		<!-- Asymmetric split-screen layout -->
		<!-- ============================================== -->
		<section class="min-h-screen grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2">
			<!-- Left: Fixed accent panel -->
			<div class="bg-gradient-to-br from-blue-950/60 via-indigo-950/40 to-[#0d0e12] p-8 lg:p-16 flex flex-col justify-center relative overflow-hidden lg:sticky lg:top-0 lg:h-screen">
				<!-- Decorative elements -->
				<div class="absolute top-0 right-0 w-64 h-64 bg-blue-500/10 rounded-full blur-[100px]"></div>
				<div class="absolute bottom-0 left-0 w-48 h-48 bg-indigo-500/10 rounded-full blur-[80px]"></div>
				
				<div class="relative z-10">
					<div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-8">
						<span class="font-oldturcic text-xl text-blue-400/40">𐰃𐰋</span>
						<span class="font-sinistre text-sm text-blue-300/60 tracking-wider">THE SPLIT</span>
					</div>
					
					<h2 class="font-sinistre text-4xl lg:text-5xl xl:text-6xl text-white leading-[1.1] mb-6">
						Thoughts on
						<span class="text-blue-300">Systems</span>
					</h2>
					
					<p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed max-w-md">
						Long-form explorations of system design, performance optimization, and the philosophy of building software that lasts.
					</p>
					
					<div class="mt-12 flex flex-col gap-2 text-sm text-gray-500">
						<span>12 articles</span>
						<span>~4 hours of reading</span>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			
			<!-- Right: Scrolling posts -->
			<div class="divide-y divide-[#90c5ff14]">
				<!-- Post 1 -->
				<article class="p-8 lg:p-12 hover:bg-[#90c5ff05] transition-colors cursor-pointer group">
					<time class="text-xs text-gray-600 font-mono">Feb 2026</time>
					<h3 class="font-sinistre text-2xl mt-2 mb-3 text-white group-hover:text-blue-200 transition-colors">
						Zero-Copy Networking in User Space
					</h3>
					<p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
						How io_uring and eBPF are changing the game for high-performance network applications.
					</p>
					<div class="mt-4 text-sm text-blue-400/50 group-hover:text-blue-400/80 transition-colors">
						18 min read
					</div>
				</article>
				
				<!-- Post 2 -->
				<article class="p-8 lg:p-12 hover:bg-[#90c5ff05] transition-colors cursor-pointer group">
					<time class="text-xs text-gray-600 font-mono">Jan 2026</time>
					<h3 class="font-sinistre text-2xl mt-2 mb-3 text-white group-hover:text-blue-200 transition-colors">
						The Database Connection Pool Problem
					</h3>
					<p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
						Why your pool is probably misconfigured and how to actually size it for your workload.
					</p>
					<div class="mt-4 text-sm text-blue-400/50 group-hover:text-blue-400/80 transition-colors">
						10 min read
					</div>
				</article>
				
				<!-- Post 3 -->
				<article class="p-8 lg:p-12 hover:bg-[#90c5ff05] transition-colors cursor-pointer group">
					<time class="text-xs text-gray-600 font-mono">Dec 2025</time>
					<h3 class="font-sinistre text-2xl mt-2 mb-3 text-white group-hover:text-blue-200 transition-colors">
						What PostgreSQL Taught Me About Consistency
					</h3>
					<p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
						MVCC, isolation levels, and the subtle bugs that hide in your understanding of transactions.
					</p>
					<div class="mt-4 text-sm text-blue-400/50 group-hover:text-blue-400/80 transition-colors">
						14 min read
					</div>
				</article>
				
				<!-- Post 4 -->
				<article class="p-8 lg:p-12 hover:bg-[#90c5ff05] transition-colors cursor-pointer group">
					<time class="text-xs text-gray-600 font-mono">Nov 2025</time>
					<h3 class="font-sinistre text-2xl mt-2 mb-3 text-white group-hover:text-blue-200 transition-colors">
						Latency: The User Experience Metric
					</h3>
					<p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
						100ms feels instant. 300ms feels sluggish. The psychology and engineering of perceived performance.
					</p>
					<div class="mt-4 text-sm text-blue-400/50 group-hover:text-blue-400/80 transition-colors">
						8 min read
					</div>
				</article>
			</div>
		</section>


		<!-- ============================================== -->
		<!-- BLOG 5: THE MARGIN -->
		<!-- Wide layout with side annotations -->
		<!-- ============================================== -->
		<section class="py-24 relative">
			<!-- Section header -->
			<div class="px-4 md:px-8 lg:px-16 mb-16">
				<div class="flex items-center gap-3">
					<span class="font-oldturcic text-xl text-blue-400/30">𐰋</span>
					<span class="font-sinistre text-sm text-gray-500 tracking-wider">THE MARGIN</span>
					<span class="flex-1 h-px bg-gradient-to-r from-[#90c5ff14] to-transparent"></span>
				</div>
			</div>
			
			<!-- Wide article with margins -->
			<article class="grid grid-cols-12 gap-0">
				<!-- Left margin annotations -->
				<div class="col-span-2 hidden lg:block">
					<div class="sticky top-24 text-right pr-8 space-y-8">
						<div class="text-xs text-gray-600">
							<div class="text-blue-400/50 font-mono mb-1">Published</div>
							<div>Feb 8, 2026</div>
						</div>
						<div class="text-xs text-gray-600">
							<div class="text-blue-400/50 font-mono mb-1">Reading</div>
							<div>22 minutes</div>
						</div>
						<div class="text-xs text-gray-600">
							<div class="text-blue-400/50 font-mono mb-1">Topics</div>
							<div class="space-y-1 mt-2">
								<div>Rust</div>
								<div>Memory</div>
								<div>Performance</div>
							</div>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>
				
				<!-- Main content -->
				<div class="col-span-12 lg:col-span-6 px-4 lg:px-0">
					<h2 class="font-sinistre text-3xl lg:text-4xl text-white mb-8 leading-tight">
						Unsafe Rust: When and Why to Cross the Line
					</h2>
					
					<div class="prose prose-invert max-w-none">
						<p class="text-lg text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
							Rust's safety guarantees are its defining feature. The borrow checker, lifetimes, the type system they all conspire to prevent entire categories of bugs at compile time. But sometimes, you need to step outside these guarantees. The question is: when is it worth it?
						</p>
						
						<h3 class="font-sinistre text-xl text-white mt-12 mb-4">The Performance Imperative</h3>
						<p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
							In our streaming platform, a critical hot path was processing 50,000 packets per second. The safe Rust version worked, but profiling revealed that bounds checks were consuming 12% of CPU time. Moving to unsafe with manual bounds verification in strategic locations cut that to 2%.
						</p>
						
						<div class="my-8 p-6 bg-blue-950/20 border-l-2 border-blue-500/50">
							<p class="text-blue-100/80 italic">
								"Unsafe doesn't mean unreviewed. Every unsafe block in our codebase requires a comment explaining the invariants being upheld."
							</p>
						</div>
						
						<h3 class="font-sinistre text-xl text-white mt-12 mb-4">The FFI Boundary</h3>
						<p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
							When calling C libraries or writing code that will be called from C, unsafe is unavoidable. The key is to wrap it in safe abstractions as quickly as possible. A thin unsafe layer with a thick safe API on top.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				
				<!-- Right margin with pull quotes and notes -->
				<div class="col-span-4 hidden lg:block">
					<div class="sticky top-24 pl-12 space-y-12">
						<!-- Pull quote -->
						<div class="border-l border-blue-500/30 pl-6">
							<p class="text-blue-200/60 italic text-sm leading-relaxed">
								"Every use of unsafe should come with a proof argument explaining why the invariants are maintained."
							</p>
						</div>
						
						<!-- Side note -->
						<div class="text-xs text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
							<div class="text-blue-400/50 font-mono mb-2">Note</div>
							<p>
								The Rust compiler team is actively working on reducing the need for unsafe in performance-critical code. Features like portable SIMD are making safe high-performance code more accessible.
							</p>
						</div>
						
						<!-- Related link -->
						<div class="text-xs">
							<div class="text-blue-400/50 font-mono mb-2">See also</div>
							<a href="#" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-blue-300 transition-colors">
								The Rustonomicon: Dark Arts of Unsafe
							</a>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>
			</article>
		</section>


		<!-- ============================================== -->
		<!-- BLOG 6: THE GRID BREAK -->
		<!-- Mixed grid with full-width breaking elements -->
		<!-- ============================================== -->
		<section class="py-20 border-t border-[#90c5ff14]">
			<div class="px-4 md:px-8 lg:px-16 mb-12">
				<div class="flex items-center gap-3">
					<span class="font-oldturcic text-xl text-blue-400/30">𐰋𐰃</span>
					<span class="font-sinistre text-sm text-gray-500 tracking-wider">THE GRID BREAK</span>
