							<h2 class="font-sinistre text-4xl lg:text-5xl text-white leading-tight mb-6">
								The Art of Deleting Code
							</h2>
							<p class="text-xl text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
								In three years, we deleted 40% of our codebase. Our velocity doubled. This is the story of learning to let go.
							</p>
						</header>
						
						<div class="prose prose-invert max-w-none">
							<p class="text-gray-300 leading-loose text-lg">
								Every line of code is a liability. It has to be understood, maintained, tested, and eventually replaced. Yet we treat code like an asset, celebrating additions and fearing deletions. This mindset is backwards.
							</p>
							
							<p class="text-gray-400 leading-loose mt-6">
								When I joined the team, our codebase was 180,000 lines. Most of it worked. Some of it was dead code nobody dared to touch. Much of it was "defensive" code written for edge cases that never materialized. The fear of deletion had created a sedimentary layer of complexity.
							</p>
							
							<!-- Inline callout box -->
							<div class="my-10 p-6 bg-[#0a0b0d] border border-[#90c5ff14] relative">
								<div class="absolute -top-3 left-6 bg-[#0d0e12] px-2 text-xs text-blue-400/60 font-mono">INSIGHT</div>
								<p class="text-gray-300">
									We measured code quality by what we removed, not what we added. Every PR that reduced net lines of code got celebrated.
								</p>
							</div>
							
							<h3 class="font-sinistre text-2xl text-white mt-12 mb-4">The Three-Month Rule</h3>
							<p class="text-gray-400 leading-loose">
								If a feature hasn't been used in three months, we delete it. No archiving, no commenting out, no "we might need it later." Delete. If we need it again, we'll build it better with fresh understanding. This simple rule eliminated 15,000 lines of code in the first quarter.
							</p>
							
							<h3 class="font-sinistre text-2xl text-white mt-12 mb-4">The Result</h3>
							<p class="text-gray-400 leading-loose">
								Today our codebase is 110,000 lines. Build times dropped 40%. New developers onboard in days, not weeks. Bug density fell by half. The code that remains is the code that matters.
							</p>
